  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1048 AM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nHigh pressure aloft and limited moisture will reduce chances for\nthunderstorm development for much of the area this afternoon and\nSaturday. Mostly likely areas for storm development will be mainly\nthe mountains of the Gila Region and the Southwest Bootheel of New\nMexico. There may be a stray isolated thunderstorms developing in\nother areas this afternoon and again to tomorrow, but coverage and\nrain potential will be very limited. Outflow winds from storms\nover the Gila region may produce strong wind gusts over portions\nof Sierra county later this afternoon and evening. Well above\nnormal temperatures area expected this afternoon and again\nSaturday and Sunday with near 100 degree highs for portions of \nthe El Paso metro and lower valley areas.   \n\nThere will be a pattern shift starting on Sunday as the high aloft\nshifts more to the east in response to a deepening low in the\nPacific northwest. This will allow a deeper plume of moisture to\nstart flow northward, spreading from west to east across New\nMexico. Sunday's storm development will principally occur west of\nthe Continental Divide and in the mountains. Monday will be\nsimilar but with additional coverage further east toward the Rio\nGrande valley.   \n\nFor Tuesday and the remainder of next week, moisture will be more\nplentiful with greater coverage of showers and thunderstorms\nexpected. Storm development and coverage may be enhanced by\nimpulses embedded in the moist flow pattern. Toward the end of the\nweek, a trough is forecasted to develop over the area that could\nset up conditions for potentially heavy rainfall and possible\nsevere thunderstorm development. Long range models remain\nuncertain run to run on the strength of this trough, but overall\nthe weather pattern for the latter half of next week looks wetter\nand more active.",
